    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> broke with Greek custom by massing the Theban infantry in a very deep column on the left, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> at least fifty shields deep, and set it against the Spartan right, where the king stood.</li><li>The ancient sources disagree on the numbers and on the course of the fighting, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, the only contemporary, does not mention <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> at all.</li><li>More than a quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s full citizens were lost, and less than a decade later Sparta had lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a> and half its territory.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 370/69 BCE a Theban-led army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia, the first invasion of the Spartan homeland by land in recorded history, and freed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">Perioeci</a> of Messenia and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> from Spartan enslavement.</p></aside>
    </section>

