    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">387/6 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a> ends the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> and guarantees the autonomy of Greek cities not under Persian control</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">382 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Spartan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoebidas">Phoebidas</a> seizes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a>, the acropolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">379 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">An anti-Spartan exile faction overthrows Leontiades at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and moves to rebuild the Boeotian League</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">371 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Thebans refuse to let each Boeotian city sign the new peace separately, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus I</a> marches into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">371 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Thebans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> defeat the Spartans and their allies at Leuctra</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">370 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> allies with Elis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and the Arcadian League</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">370/69 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A Theban-led army invades <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia and frees the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">Perioeci</a> of Messenia and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">362 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> wins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-362-bce">Second Battle of Mantinea</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> dies, and its hegemony ends</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-leuctra" data-v="4eb60d502c" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies draw up</b> The Spartans stood with their own troops on the right and their allies on the left. Epaminondas set his Thebans in a very deep column on the left, opposite the Spartan right, with the other Boeotians further right and back.</li><li><b>The cavalry meet and the Spartan horse is beaten</b> The battle opened with a cavalry engagement between the lines. The Theban cavalry, the more experienced of the two, quickly defeated the Spartan cavalry.</li><li><b>The Spartan cavalry flees and the Thebans charge</b> The Spartan cavalry fled back through its own infantry and disordered the Spartan line just as the Thebans charged. The other Boeotians held back, by one account withdrawing gradually as they were attacked.</li><li><b>The Spartan right holds while the Thebans press it</b> The Spartan line held long enough for the mortally wounded Cleombrotus to be carried off the field still alive. The massed Thebans, with the Sacred Band in front, pressed against the Spartan right.</li><li><b>The Spartan right falls back</b> The Spartan right wing then fell back under the pressure of the Theban mass, which kept pushing against it with the Sacred Band in front.</li><li><b>The Spartan right wing is turned and routed</b> The Spartan right wing was pushed back, then turned, and in the end it was routed and fled. Many of the Spartan hippeis, the royal bodyguard, had been killed in the fighting.</li><li><b>The Spartan left wing gives way</b> The men on the left wing of the Spartan army saw that the right wing was being pushed back and gave way, so both wings of the Spartan army had given way.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corinthian-war.jpg" alt="Corinthian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grave_relief_of_Dexileos,_son_of_Lysanias,_of_Thorikos_(Ca._390_BC)_(4454389225).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tilemahos Efthimiadis from Athens, Greece, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a>, fought from 395 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> and a Persian-backed coalition of Thebes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, Corinth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and other allies, ended in 387/6 BCE with the King's Peace, also called the Peace of Antalcidas or the first Common Peace. It guaranteed the autonomy of all Greek cities not under Persian control. Sparta, as guarantor of the peace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, used the autonomy clause to force the Thebes-dominated Boeotian League to disband. In 382 BCE the Spartan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoebidas">Phoebidas</a> seized the Theban acropolis, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a>, helping the pro-Spartan Leontiades and his oligarchic supporters take control of Thebes, a clear violation of Theban autonomy.</p><p>In late 379 BCE an anti-Spartan Theban exile faction overthrew Leontiades and showed its intent to reconstitute the Boeotian League by electing four of its leaders Boeotarchs. The Boeotian War began in 378 BCE, and Sparta's attempts to regain control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> failed in succession. A short-lived peace was agreed in 375 BCE, with renewed aggression by Thebes in 373 BCE. Thebes captured and severely damaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> and Thespiae and forced their citizens into exile.</p><p>By 371 BCE both Sparta and Thebes' ally Athens were ready for peace after costly naval campaigns. Thebes, facing the prospect of fighting Sparta alone, was prepared to agree to a new treaty promising the independence of all Greek cities. At the signing the Theban ambassadors wished to sign for the whole Boeotian League, but the Spartans demanded that each Boeotian city sign independently, and the Thebans refused.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus.jpg" alt="Diodorus Siculus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diodoro_siculo_-_storico_di_Agira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Several ancient sources give numbers for the two armies, but none gives all of them and they nearly all disagree. The general modern consensus is that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus</a> led approximately 10,000 Peloponnesian infantry, including 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a> (full Spartan citizens), and 1,000 cavalry, against 7,000 Boeotian infantry and 700 cavalry under the Theban commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> in command of the elite Sacred Band of 300. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> has Epaminondas lead no more than 6,000, to be joined later by 1,500 Thessalian infantry and 500 cavalry. Later sources give much higher figures for the Peloponnesians: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polyaenus">Polyaenus</a> 40,000 against 6,000 Thebans, Frontinus 24,000 infantry and 1,600 cavalry against 3,600 infantry and 400 cavalry.</p><p>Greek heavy infantry normally formed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, a solid rectangular mass in long files, with eight ranks thought a common depth. The most experienced troops went on the right wing. Cleombrotus drew up two wings, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> not more than twelve men deep, with the Spartans on the right and the allies on the left. He took the king's traditional place between the first and second morai of the Spartan wing, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippeis">hippeis</a>, a royal bodyguard of 300 young men.</p><p>Epaminondas also formed two wings, the Thebans on one and the other Boeotians on the other. Breaking with tradition, he massed the Thebans in a very deep column on the left, opposite Cleombrotus. The other Boeotians stood in a shallower echelon, progressively further to the right and rear, facing the Spartan allies. His plan was apparently to make the outcome depend on the Thebans against the Spartans, holding back his allies. No source reports where exactly the Sacred Band stood.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xenophon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xenophon.jpg" alt="Xenophon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Xenophon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, the only contemporary source, while both sides were still arming, the Spartans' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> peltasts attacked Boeotian camp followers and baggage handlers who were trying to withdraw, driving them back into the Boeotian army and so making it stronger. The battle proper opened with a cavalry engagement in which the Spartan cavalry was quickly defeated. It fled back through its own infantry, disrupting the Spartan lines just as the Thebans charged. Pausanias says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a>, suspecting that some Boeotian allies, particularly the Thespians, might desert, declared that all who did not wish to fight could leave, and the Thespians and some other non-Theban Boeotians did so.</p><p>Diodorus says both sides attacked together at the sound of the trumpets, the Spartans in a crescent with their left and right advancing faster than their centre, the Theban wing at double-quick time while the weaker Boeotian wing retreated. The fighting was at first even. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus</a> fell, and although the Spartans recovered his body, they were forced back by the heavy column while still holding formation, and finally fled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says Cleombrotus was extending his right wing to encircle Epaminondas, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> charged with the 300 before the line had been closed up again.</p><p>The massed Thebans, with the Sacred Band in front, struck the Spartan right wing, mortally wounded Cleombrotus and killed many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippeis">hippeis</a>. The Spartan wing was pushed back, then turned and routed. Xenophon says the Spartan line held long enough for the king to be carried off alive, and the left wing gave way when it saw the right pushed back. Pausanias says the allies gave way wherever they were attacked, and that the Spartans could not retreat once their king had fallen.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aristotle.jpg" alt="Aristotle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Lysippos, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> the Lacedaemonians, meaning the Spartans and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">Perioeci</a>, lost almost 1,000 dead, including about 400 of the 700 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spartiates">Spartiates</a> present. Boeotian losses were about 300 according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, while Pausanias reports a total of only 47. Both sides sent messengers asking for reinforcements. A truce was agreed so that the armies could collect their dead, the Thebans set up a tropaion, and the two armies held their positions for several days.</p><p>According to Xenophon, when the Spartan messenger reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> all men under sixty were mobilised and sent to Leuctra under Archidamus, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a>. The Athenians dismissed the Theban messenger unanswered, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>' ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason-of-pherae">Jason of Pherae</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> led an army south at once. When he arrived the Thebans proposed a joint attack on the Spartan position, but Jason convinced them to be content with their victory and to let the Spartans withdraw under truce.</p><p>More than a quarter of Sparta's population of full citizens had been lost, compounding an already depleted citizen body. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> the battle was the single blow that ended Sparta's dominance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Sparta had tried to cover up earlier battlefield losses, but at Leuctra the size of the defeat was apparent to all.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Four ancient writers give accounts: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>, the only contemporary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> in the later first century BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> around 100 CE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pausanias-geographer">Pausanias</a> in the late second century CE. Their accounts are sometimes contradictory and many particulars have been debated by scholars. Xenophon's is the most complete and has details found in no other source, but it consists less of a narrative than of reasons, some might say excuses, for the surprising Spartan defeat, and it cites an oracle and omens that pointed to a Theban victory. He says nothing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> or of Theban tactics. No other source mentions the attack on the withdrawing camp followers or a cavalry engagement.</p><p>Diodorus and Plutarch describe Epaminondas's plan of an elite wing and a weaker wing held back, and the sources give different reasons for the disorder in the Spartan line: Xenophon the fleeing cavalry, Plutarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus</a>'s attempt to extend his wing. Plutarch and Cornelius Nepos are the sources for the part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> and the Sacred Band. Köchly and Rüstow, writing in 1852, believed that Pelopidas led the Sacred Band out from the rear of the Theban column to attack the Spartan right in the flank. Hans Delbrück disputed this in 1920, finding nothing in Plutarch about a breaking out to the flank, and his rejection has found near-universal agreement among modern scholars. The view that the Sacred Band fought as an independent unit in front rests on Plutarch's remark about Pelopidas after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tegyra">Battle of Tegyra</a> in 375 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For nearly two centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> had been a Greek superpower, and in 404 BCE, after its Persian-aided victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, it had become the undisputed leader of the Greek world. After Leuctra its hegemony was broken. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, democratic factions in cities long dominated by Sparta revived, leading to civil strife, local power struggles and shifting alliances. Less than a decade after Leuctra Sparta had lost its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a> and half its territory.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> was the principal beneficiary, and the period 371–362 BCE is widely known as the Theban hegemony. It extended its power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and in 370 BCE allied with Elis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and the Arcadian League, which had seceded from the Peloponnesian League. In the winter of 370/69 BCE a Theban-led army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> and Messenia. Further invasions in 369 and 366 BCE won Sikyon, Achaean Pellene and all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-362-bce">Second Battle of Mantinea</a> in 362 BCE, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> died, marked the end of the Theban hegemony. Less than thirty years later Thebes suffered utter destruction at the hands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>.</p><p>The Theban victory is commonly attributed to innovations of Epaminondas: the concentration of elite forces on the left, an oblique attack, a refused right wing, forces held in reserve and the integrated use of cavalry and infantry. How revolutionary these were is debatable, but they are commonly thought to have influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>, who lived in Thebes as a hostage between 368 and 365 BCE. Pausanias called it the most famous victory ever won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> over Greeks.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 6 July 371 BCE the Thebans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> defeated a Spartan army under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleombrotus-i">Cleombrotus</a> at Leuctra after a dispute over renewing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> lost its hegemony over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> held the leading place for about nine years.</em></p>

